   "input": "/bin/zsh -c 'cat /var/folders/3g/vf36b0b14wzd0fgsgtdp06h00000gp/T/writing-probe/p-84edf890/bcda1010.txt'",
   "output": "Read the file `/var/folders/3g/vf36b0b14wzd0fgsgtdp06h00000gp/T/writing-probe/g-6f13cf5f/09888beb.md` and follow it (skip this step when the file is empty). Read no other file. Write the landing page copy for `sackview`, following the tool page section order. Use only the facts in the fact sheet below; do not add facts. Reply with the page copy as Markdown and nothing else. Do not edit files, start subagents, or browse.\n\nFact sheet:\n\n1. sackview shows what is inside a ZIP archive before you unpack it.\n2. Each entry shows its name, compressed size, and unpacked size.\n3. Install: `pipx install sackview`.\n4. `sackview inspect photos.zip` lists the archive's entries.\n5. `sackview inspect photos.zip --largest 10` shows the ten entries with the largest unpacked sizes, largest first.\n6. `sackview inspect photos.zip --find '*.csv'` shows only entries whose names match the pattern.\n7. It displays the total unpacked size so you can check available disk space before extracting elsewhere.\n8. It never extracts entries or changes the archive.\n9. Inspection works offline and sends no archive contents to a service.\n10. It supports ZIP archives only; other archive formats produce an unsupported-format message.\n11. Entry metadata is read through the internal ZipDirectoryReader adapter.\n12. Size totals use unsigned 64-bit accumulators.\n13. Unsupported formats return exit code 24.\n14. The package build copies its command schema from packaging/commands.json.\n",
